Package: vtun
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Description: virtual tunnel over TCP/IP networks (OpenSSL 3 / modern-system fork)
 VTun (vtund) creates virtual tunnels over TCP/IP networks, supporting IP,
 Ethernet, serial and pipe tunnels with traffic shaping, compression and
 encryption. This is a maintained fork that makes vtun build and run on
 modern systems: OpenSSL 3.x, GCC 14+, Debian 13 "trixie" and Ubuntu 24.04,
 where vtun is no longer packaged.
 .
 The key fix targets the OpenSSL 3 transition. Blowfish is vtun's default
 cipher ("encrypt yes" == blowfish128ecb), and on OpenSSL 3 Blowfish moved
 into the "legacy" provider, which is not loaded by default. Pristine vtun
 ignored the resulting cipher-init failure and dereferenced a half-initialised
 context, segfaulting inside libcrypto.so.3 the moment a Blowfish session
 connected. Because vtund forks one child per session, only the child died
 while the master kept logging "waiting for connections", so the crash looked
 like clients randomly failing to connect. This fork loads the OpenSSL 3
 legacy provider in-process (before daemonising, so it survives the
 environment-stripping fork) and ports the EVP cipher code to the opaque
 OpenSSL 1.1+/3.x API. It also fixes the non-ECB modes (CBC/CFB/OFB) by
 including <arpa/inet.h> for a correct htonl() prototype, builds cleanly on
 GCC 14, and repairs fork detection for standalone-server (-s) mode.
 .
 Supported ciphers are Blowfish and AES (128- and 256-bit) in ECB/CBC/CFB/OFB
 modes. For Blowfish sessions the OpenSSL legacy provider must be present at
 runtime; on Debian/Ubuntu it ships as ossl-modules/legacy.so in the standard
 libssl3 package, so normally nothing extra needs to be installed. AES
 sessions need only the default provider.
 .
 Security note: Blowfish has a 64-bit block size (Sweet32, CVE-2016-2183) and
 the default uses ECB mode (leaks repeated plaintext blocks); prefer
 "encrypt aes256cbc" for new setups. vtun has no AEAD, no message
 authentication and no forward secrecy; for greenfield deployments a modern
 VPN such as WireGuard is the better choice. This fork exists to keep existing
 vtun deployments working on modern systems, especially hubs whose legacy
 peers can only speak vtun.
